From 8da5f3347091e1afa0d425ebb3d7e75de648f689 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:22:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(render): CAM-02 - the mip pyramid that stopped four
 levels short
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The photographs of our five species have been in the repository since
VIS-07 and the renderer has never once sampled them. makeGrainTexture is
exported and called from nowhere, so options.grain is always undefined and
woodPalette has always fallen back to the two-hex-colour drawn path. That
is the whole reason /design looks like a gradient: there is a photograph of
white oak sitting in public/textures and no wire from it to the renderer.

Wiring it in naively made the floor WORSE, and this commit is the reason
why. Measured on a neighbour-delta speckle metric, higher being noisier:

    drawn (what ships)        2.92
    photographic, as wired   19.01

Six times noisier than the thing it was meant to improve on. Two causes,
both of them arithmetic:

  1. THE MIP LEVEL WAS RELATIVE, NOT ABSOLUTE. It came from the ratio of
     the projective divisor against the divisor at the near edge of the
     quad - level 0 at the bottom of the frame, rising towards the wall.
     Right shape, wrong origin. The near edge of a room photograph is not
     where one screen pixel covers one texel. Measured on the studio
     scene: a pixel there covers 0.57 inches of floor, the tile measures
     ten inches across 512 texels, so it covers 29 texels and wants level
     4.86. At the wall line it covers 102 texels and wants 6.67. The whole
     pyramid was offset by nearly five levels for every pixel in the frame.

  2. THE PYRAMID STOPPED AT LEVEL 4. Even with the offset corrected there
     was no level 5 or 6 to ask for. A pyramid that bottoms out before the
     scene does is the same aliasing as no pyramid, only quieter.

So the level is now measured, not inferred. The footprint of one screen
pixel on the floor comes from the homography's own derivatives -
d(plan)/d(n) = (m_ij - plan*m_2j) / w, and w was already computed to
project the point, so this is two divisions and four multiplies with no
second sample and no screen-space derivative pass. The larger of the two
screen axes decides, because the smaller one under-filters along exactly
the direction the floor is running away in. grainAt turns that footprint
into a level using the tile's own texels-per-inch, which is where that
knowledge belongs.

Result, same metric, far field: 3.62 against the drawn path's 3.64. The
photographic floor is now as quiet as the drawn one and carries the real
grain. The drawn path is byte-for-byte unchanged - all four patterns, all
three species compared, max absolute channel difference zero.

Two more defects found on the way:

  · PlankSample now reports lenIn, the board's own length in inches.
    `along` is a FRACTION and a fraction is not a distance; the inches it
    stands for are the fraction times the length it is a fraction OF, and
    that is nine widths for a straight run but four for a herringbone or
    chevron block. Reading nine everywhere stretched the photograph along
    a parquet block by 9/4.

  · Trilinear between the two levels either side, not a snap to the
    nearer. Snapping puts a hard line across the floor wherever the level
    changes, which on a photograph of a room reads as a crease in the
    floor. One extra bilinear fetch removes it.

  · makeGrainTexture's box filter clamped its second tap, so a tile with
    an odd or non-square dimension filters correctly instead of reading
    past the row.

Tested with a low-contrast grating rather than a photograph: it needs no
image decoder, and a checkerboard is the wrong probe twice over - bilinear
reconstruction of a one-texel checker is itself an aliasing pattern, and
its contrast sits on the detail clamp, so the measurement moves with the
sampling grid instead of the filter. The ladder the tests assert:

    footprint (in/px)  0.01  0.05  0.10  0.25  0.50  1.00  2.00  4.00
    detail surviving  47.9  47.9  47.9  41.3  21.4   5.7   1.7   1.7

flat while the pixel is inside one texel, monotonically down after, and
settling at 1.77 - which is the drawn grain, with the photograph filtered
entirely away. The test asserts against that number rather than against
zero, because the drawn sine waves have no footprint term and a bare
threshold here would be measuring them and calling it the filter.

parse-scan clean on 634 files. 64 of 66 guards pass; verify:css cannot
run without an install and fails identically on a clean checkout, and
verify:hygiene is the two transport envelopes still at the repository
root.

This does not yet change what /design draws - the wire from the browser to
makeGrainTexture is the next commit. It makes the renderer able to carry a
photograph without turning it into static, which it could not do before.

